Installing Helm
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand what Helm is and its role in Kubernetes package management
Install Helm on a Linux system
Configure Helm to work with a Kubernetes cluster
Verify Helm installation and functionality
Use basic Helm commands to interact with Kubernetes
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Fundamental knowledge of Kubernetes concepts (pods, services, deployments)
Familiarity with YAML file structure
Basic understanding of package managers
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided Linux machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will need to install all required tools during this lab exercise.

What is Helm?
Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes, often referred to as the "apt/yum for Kubernetes." It helps you manage Kubernetes applications through Helm Charts, which are collections of files that describe a related set of Kubernetes resources.

Task 1: Setting Up the Environment
Subtask 1.1: Update System Packages
First, let's ensure our system is up to date:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
Subtask 1.2: Install Required Dependencies
Install curl and other essential tools:

sudo apt install -y curl wget apt-transport-https ca-certificates gnupg lsb-release
Subtask 1.3: Install Docker
Since we need Kubernetes, let's install Docker first:

# Add Docker's official GPG key
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg

# Add Docker repository
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null

# Update package index
sudo apt update

# Install Docker
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io

# Add current user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# Start and enable Docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
Log out and log back in, or run:

newgrp docker
Verify Docker installation:

docker --version
Subtask 1.4: Install kubectl
Install the Kubernetes command-line tool:

# Download kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"

# Make it executable
chmod +x kubectl

# Move to system path
sudo mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/

# Verify installation
kubectl version --client
Subtask 1.5: Install Minikube
For this lab, we'll use Minikube to create a local Kubernetes cluster:

# Download Minikube
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64

# Make it executable
chmod +x minikube-linux-amd64

# Move to system path
sudo mv minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube

# Verify installation
minikube version
Subtask 1.6: Start Kubernetes Cluster
Start a local Kubernetes cluster using Minikube:

# Start Minikube with Docker driver
minikube start --driver=docker

# Verify cluster is running
kubectl cluster-info

# Check nodes
kubectl get nodes
Task 2: Installing Helm
Subtask 2.1: Download and Install Helm
There are multiple ways to install Helm. We'll use the official installation script:

# Download and run the Helm installation script
curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash
Alternative manual installation method:

# Download the latest Helm release
wget https://get.helm.sh/helm-v3.13.0-linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
tar -zxvf helm-v3.13.0-linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Move helm binary to system path
sudo mv linux-amd64/helm /usr/local/bin/helm

# Clean up
rm -rf linux-amd64 helm-v3.13.0-linux-amd64.tar.gz
Subtask 2.2: Verify Helm Installation
Check if Helm is installed correctly:

# Check Helm version
helm version

# Check Helm help
helm help
You should see output similar to:

version.BuildInfo{Version:"v3.13.0", GitCommit:"825e86f6a7a38cef1112bfa606e4127a706749b1", GitTreeState:"clean", GoVersion:"go1.20.8"}
Task 3: Configuring Helm with Kubernetes
Subtask 3.1: Verify Kubernetes Connection
Ensure Helm can communicate with your Kubernetes cluster:

# Check current Kubernetes context
kubectl config current-context

# Verify Helm can connect to Kubernetes
helm list
The helm list command should run without errors, even if it returns an empty list.

Subtask 3.2: Add Helm Repositories
Helm uses repositories to store charts. Let's add some popular repositories:

# Add the official Helm stable repository
helm repo add stable https://charts.helm.sh/stable

# Add Bitnami repository (popular for applications)
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami

# Update repository information
helm repo update

# List added repositories
helm repo list
Subtask 3.3: Search for Available Charts
Let's explore what charts are available:

# Search for nginx charts
helm search repo nginx

# Search for all charts in bitnami repository
helm search repo bitnami

# Get more detailed information about a specific chart
helm show chart bitnami/nginx
Task 4: Testing Helm Installation
Subtask 4.1: Install a Test Application
Let's install a simple nginx application to test our Helm setup:

# Install nginx using Helm
helm install my-nginx bitnami/nginx

# Check the installation status
helm status my-nginx

# List all Helm releases
helm list
Subtask 4.2: Verify the Deployment
Check if the application was deployed successfully:

# Check pods
kubectl get pods

# Check services
kubectl get services

# Get detailed information about the nginx service
kubectl describe service my-nginx
Subtask 4.3: Access the Application
Since we're using Minikube, let's access the nginx service:

# Get the service URL
minikube service my-nginx --url
Open another terminal and test the connection:

# Test the nginx service (replace URL with the one from previous command)
curl $(minikube service my-nginx --url)
You should see the nginx welcome page HTML.

Subtask 4.4: Explore Helm Commands
Let's explore some useful Helm commands:

# Get release history
helm history my-nginx

# Get release values
helm get values my-nginx

# Get all information about the release
helm get all my-nginx

# Show the chart information
helm show chart bitnami/nginx

# Show the chart values
helm show values bitnami/nginx
Subtask 4.5: Upgrade and Rollback
Let's practice upgrading and rolling back releases:

# Upgrade the release with custom values
helm upgrade my-nginx bitnami/nginx --set service.type=NodePort

# Check the upgrade
helm history my-nginx

# Rollback to previous version
helm rollback my-nginx 1

# Verify rollback
helm history my-nginx
Subtask 4.6: Clean Up
Remove the test application:

# Uninstall the release
helm uninstall my-nginx

# Verify removal
helm list
kubectl get pods
Task 5: Advanced Helm Configuration
Subtask 5.1: Create a Custom Values File
Create a custom values file for more complex configurations:

# Create a custom values file
cat > custom-nginx-values.yaml << EOF
replicaCount: 2

service:
  type: NodePort
  port: 80

resources:
  limits:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 128Mi
  requests:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 128Mi

ingress:
  enabled: false
EOF
Subtask 5.2: Install with Custom Values
Install nginx using the custom values file:

# Install with custom values
helm install custom-nginx bitnami/nginx -f custom-nginx-values.yaml

# Verify the installation
kubectl get pods
kubectl get services
Subtask 5.3: Template Rendering
See what Kubernetes manifests Helm would generate:

# Render templates without installing
helm template custom-nginx bitnami/nginx -f custom-nginx-values.yaml

# Save rendered templates to a file
helm template custom-nginx bitnami/nginx -f custom-nginx-values.yaml > rendered-manifests.yaml

# View the file
cat rendered-manifests.yaml
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Permission Denied Errors
If you encounter permission errors with Docker:

# Add user to docker group and refresh
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker
Issue 2: Minikube Won't Start
If Minikube fails to start:

# Delete existing cluster and restart
minikube delete
minikube start --driver=docker --force
Issue 3: Helm Repository Errors
If repository operations fail:

# Remove and re-add repositories
helm repo remove stable
helm repo remove bitnami
helm repo add stable https://charts.helm.sh/stable
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update
Issue 4: kubectl Connection Issues
If kubectl cannot connect to the cluster:

# Reset kubectl context
minikube update-context
kubectl config current-context
